# Git Ignore Hands-On Lab

Objective:
The objective of this hands-on is to understand the use of .gitignore file and learn how to ignore unwanted files and folders from being tracked by Git.

Software Used:
Git Bash
GitHub
Notepad++

Procedure:

1. Opened the existing Git repository.
Commands:
cd OneDrive
cd Desktop
cd GitDemo

2. Created a sample log file.
Command:
echo "This is a sample log file" > sample.log

3. Created a log folder.
Command:
mkdir log

4. Created a .gitignore file.
Command:
touch .gitignore

5. Opened the .gitignore file using Notepad.
Command:
notepad .gitignore
Added the following lines in the .gitignore file.

*.log
log/

Saved the file and closed Notepad.

6. Checked the repository status.
Command:
git status
Verified that sample.log and log folder were ignored and only .gitignore was shown as an untracked file.

7. Added the .gitignore file to the staging area.
Command:
git add .gitignore

8. Checked the status again.
Command:
git status

9. Committed the changes.
Command:
git commit

Commit Message:
Added .gitignore file

10. Pushed the changes to GitHub.
Command:
git push

Result:
Successfully created a .gitignore file to ignore .log files and log folders. Verified using git status that the ignored files were not tracked by Git and pushed the changes successfully to GitHub.
